<p>Here&rsquo;s a niche problem: When viewing iPhone-captured photos in org-mode on Linux, they always appear in landscape orientation, even if you took them in portrait.</p>
<p>The reason is that the sensor of the camera on the phone is physically embedded in landscape mode, so all photos are in landscape mode; when you hold the phone in portrait mode (which is how you hold it most of the time), the phone detects that and implements a fix in the EXIF data file. Essentially, a software fix to a hardware design issue.</p>
<p>Since most photos I take are usually in portrait orientation, it means I need to twist my neck and view images at a 90-degree angle when I look at my Journelly entries.</p>
<p>The source of the problem seems to stem from how org-mode interprets EXIF data in the photo: it doesn&rsquo;t. It relies on other parts of Emacs, which in turn rely on parts of the OS, to do the job. On Linux (at least on Kubuntu, which is what I use these days), those parts don&rsquo;t handle EXIF orientation information. Why and how, I am not sure, it&rsquo;s more digging than I have the time for right now… but anyway - there&rsquo;s a simple fix.</p>
<p><a href="https://imagemagick.org/command-line-options/#auto-orient">Auto-orient</a>, which is part of <a href="https://imagemagick.org/mogrify/">ImageMagick&rsquo;s mogrify tool</a>. And if you use Emacs on Linux, good chance it&rsquo;s already installed:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This operator reads and resets the EXIF image profile setting &lsquo;Orientation&rsquo; and then performs the appropriate 90 degree rotation on the image to orient the image, for correct viewing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To execute on an image file: <code>mogrify -auto-orient &lt;file&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>And because I use Emacs, of course there&rsquo;s a <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/dwim-shell-command">dwim-shell-command</a> solution:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"><code class="language-elisp" data-lang="elisp"><span style="display:flex;"><span>    (defun jtr/dwim-image-auto-orient ()
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>      <span style="color:#e6db74">&#34;Auto-orient images based on EXIF data using mogrify.&#34;</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>      (interactive)
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>      (dwim-shell-command-on-marked-files
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>       <span style="color:#e6db74">&#34;Auto-orient images&#34;</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>       <span style="color:#e6db74">&#34;mogrify -auto-orient &#39;&lt;&lt;f&gt;&gt;&#39;&#34;</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>       :utils <span style="color:#e6db74">&#34;mogrify&#34;</span>
</span></span><span style="display:flex;"><span>       :silent-success <span style="color:#66d9ef">t</span>))
</span></span></code></pre></div><p>That last part,<code>:silent-success</code>, closes the empty buffer that pops up after successful execution, as mogrify doesn&rsquo;t really produce an output window. So, it will just bring us back to Dired.</p>
